4.4 Flanking Logic
Flanking is a core autonomous behavior. Squads identify weak angles and maneuver to exploit them.

Flanking Conditions
A flank is attempted when:

Enemy is entrenched

Direct assault is high‑risk

Terrain allows side approach

Suppression zones block frontal attack

Squad personality supports maneuvering

Flanking Steps
Identify weak angle

Pathfind around suppression zones

Maintain cover

Coordinate with main assault

Engage from side or rear

Flanking is fully autonomous and requires no micromanagement.

4.5 Breach Logic
Breach behavior is one of the most advanced systems in Obsidian Protocol.

Breach Team Composition
Stack Team — forms up at breach point

Suppress Team — lays down covering fire

Entry Team — enters and clears

Anchor Team — holds perimeter

Breach Steps
Identify breach point

Stack formation

Suppression begins

Entry team moves

Room clearing

Consolidation

Expansion or fallback

Dynamic Adjustments
If suppression fails or casualties rise:

Breach is aborted

Squad retreats

Squad switches to hold intent

Squad requests support

4.6 Suppression Response
Squads react intelligently to suppression.

Suppression Effects
Reduced accuracy

Reduced movement speed

Increased retreat likelihood

Increased cover‑seeking behavior

Suppression Avoidance
Squads will:

Path around suppression zones

Crawl or crouch behind cover

Deploy smoke or countermeasures

Call for support

Flank instead of assault

